 ## Overview
-This method converts OpenVDB volumetric datasets into a set of non-overlapping 3D Gaussian particles to enable memory-efficient, lossy representation with Level of Detail (LOD) support [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large sparse volumetric datasets in real-time by replacing dense voxel grids with hardware-accelerated ray tracing of Gaussian primitives [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard Gaussian Splatting which often uses overlapping primitives for photorealism, this approach strictly maintains non-overlapping Gaussians derived from OpenVDB's non-overlapping leaf nodes, limiting the model to specific refinement levels but ensuring compatibility with traditional volume rendering pipelines via transfer functions [S1, unofficial].
+This method converts OpenVDB volumetric datasets into a set of non-overlapping 3D Gaussian particles to enable memory-efficient, lossy representation with Level of Detail (LOD) support [S1, unofficial]. It renders volumetric and level-set data using forward ray marching through Gaussian primitives via NVIDIA OptiX [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard Gaussian Splatting which often uses overlapping primitives for photorealism, this approach strictly maintains non-overlapping Gaussians derived from OpenVDB's non-overlapping leaf nodes, limiting the model to specific refinement levels but ensuring compatibility with traditional volume rendering pipelines via transfer functions [S1, unofficial].
 
